.\" Manual page for pam_ssoossh.
.\" Rendered with: man -l docs/pam_ssoossh.8
.TH PAM_SSOOSSH 8 "2026-09-04" "pam_ssoossh" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
pam_ssoossh \- authenticate by having a human approve a certificate request
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B auth sufficient pam_ssoossh.so
.RI "server=" URL
.RI "trusted-ca-file=" PATH
.RI [ options ]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pam_ssoossh
authenticates a local account by generating a single-use SSH keypair,
asking
.BR ssoosshd (8)
to certify it, showing the person at the terminal how to approve the
request, and validating the certificate that comes back.
.PP
It implements the
.B auth
stack only, through
.B pam_sm_authenticate
and
.BR pam_sm_setcred .
The latter establishes nothing: the certificate is consumed during
authentication and never installed into the session.
.PP
Two flows exist and
.B mode=auto
picks between them per login.
The
.I browser
flow prints an approval URL, which suits any terminal someone can click or
copy from.
The
.I console
flow prints a short code and a QR code instead, for a machine with no
browser in front of it \(em a virtual console, a serial line, a BMC viewer.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI server= URL
Required. The
.B ssoosshd
base URL. A missing scheme becomes
.BR https:// ,
and trailing slashes are trimmed. Absent or empty is
.BR PAM_USER_UNKNOWN ,
returned before any key is generated or any socket opened.
.TP
.BI trusted-ca-file= PATH
Required. CA public keys in
.BR authorized_keys (5)
format, one per line, so a deployment can rotate CAs without a coordinated
restart. A key of an algorithm this build cannot verify is skipped with a
warning naming it and the line; if none usable remains, that is
.BR PAM_NO_MODULE_DATA ,
the same code an unreadable file produces.
.TP
.BI principals-map= PATH
Optional. Maps a local account to the certificate principals allowed to
assume it \(em see
.B PRINCIPALS MAP
below. A map that is configured but fails to load logs a warning and falls
back to requiring the certificate to carry the exact account name. It does
not fail the login: a typo'd path degrades to the stricter default rather
than locking every account out of the host.
.TP
.BI skew-tolerance= DURATION
Default
.BR 2s .
Applied symmetrically to both ends of the certificate's validity window, to
absorb clock skew between the issuing server and this host.
.TP
.BI timeout= DURATION
Default
.BR 60s .
Bounds the whole attempt, not only the waiting half. The server's own
.B expires_at
bounds it further when it is the earlier of the two.
.TP
.B insecure-skip-verify
Default off. Disables TLS verification of the
.B ssoosshd
certificate. With this on, nothing the server says is authenticated,
including the URL that reaches the terminal; the module logs a warning on
every attempt that uses it.
.TP
.BI mode= auto | sudo | console
Default
.BR auto .
.B auto
runs the console flow when
.B PAM_RHOST
is empty and
.B PAM_TTY
names a physical terminal \(em a virtual console, a serial line, a
hypervisor console, or
.B /dev/console
\(em and the browser flow otherwise. A pseudo-terminal is a terminal
emulator or an SSH session, where a browser is a keystroke away, so it is
not a console. An unset
.B PAM_TTY
is a cron job or a script and is not one either.
.B sudo
and
.B console
force a flow. An unrecognized value is
.BR PAM_NO_MODULE_DATA ,
never a silent fall back.
Console mode is compiled in on Linux and FreeBSD only; on macOS,
.B mode=console
is refused and
.B mode=auto
uses the browser flow.
.TP
.B debug
Default off. Logs the decision for each of the four checks at
.BR LOG_DEBUG .
The legacy value
.B debug=stdout
is accepted and treated as plain
.BR debug :
writing to a stream that belongs to
.BR sudo (8)
is the one thing this module never does.
.PP
Durations use Go's
.B time.ParseDuration
grammar \(em
.BR 2s ", " 500ms ", " 1h30m ", " 1.5h
\(em because that is what existing
.BR pam.d (5)
lines already contain. An unparseable duration is silently the default.
.SH RETURN VALUES
.TP
.B PAM_SUCCESS
The certificate passed all four checks.
.TP
.B PAM_USER_UNKNOWN
.B pam_get_user
failed, or
.B server
is not configured.
.TP
.B PAM_NO_MODULE_DATA
.I argv
could not be read, the trusted CA file is missing or unusable, or an
argument is malformed.
.TP
.B PAM_AUTH_ERR
Denied, expired, failed to issue, unparseable, or any check failing. Also a
timeout.
.TP
.B PAM_IGNORE
The person pressed Ctrl-C at the approval prompt. The module contributes
nothing and the stack continues to whatever follows it.
.TP
.B PAM_AUTHINFO_UNAVAIL
.B ssoosshd
could not be reached. This lets the stack fall through, so an ssoossh
outage does not become a
.BR sudo (8)
outage on every host. A server that answered and said no is
.B PAM_AUTH_ERR
instead.
.TP
.B PAM_ABORT
The keypair or the HTTP client could not be constructed.
.SH THE FOUR CHECKS
Run in order; every failure produces
.B PAM_AUTH_ERR
and logs its reason at
.BR LOG_ERR .
.IP 1. 4
.B CA signature
\(em the certificate is cryptographically signed by a key in
.BR trusted-ca-file .
Not a string comparison against that file's contents.
.IP 2. 4
.B Key binding
\(em the certificate's public key is the one generated for this attempt.
Without it, checks 1, 3 and 4 passing together would accept any CA-signed
certificate carrying the right principal, including one issued to somebody
else's keypair.
.IP 3. 4
.B Principal
\(em the certificate's principals authorize this local account.
.IP 4. 4
.B Validity window
\(em now is within
.RB [ valid_after ", " valid_before ]
plus or minus
.BR skew-tolerance .
.SH PRINCIPALS MAP
A fixed subset of YAML, parsed without a YAML library:
.PP
.RS
.nf
# who may become each account
alice:
  - alice
  - admin
bob: [bob, ops]
carol:          # null and ~ mean the same: nobody
.fi
.RE
.PP
An account with no entry is never allowed, even when a certificate
principal happens to match its name. Quoted values have their quotes
stripped; escapes are not interpreted and a value containing one is
rejected rather than guessed at.
.SH CERTIFICATE ALGORITHMS
Which CA key types work is decided by the crypto the platform ships.
Anything unsupported fails with an error naming the algorithm, never with a
vague signature failure.
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l l.
CA key type@Linux, FreeBSD@macOS
_
ecdsa\-sha2\-nistp256/384/521@yes@yes
rsa\-sha2\-256, rsa\-sha2\-512@yes@yes
ssh\-ed25519@yes@no
ssh\-rsa (SHA\-1)@refused@refused
.TE
.PP
.B ssh-rsa
names RSA with SHA\-1, which OpenSSH has disabled by default since 8.8. It
is refused by policy on every platform. macOS has no C\-callable Ed25519 \(em
Apple's is in CryptoKit, which is Swift\-only \(em so an Ed25519 CA cannot be
used there.
.PP
The per\-attempt keypair is always ECDSA P\-384, which every backend
supports, so the table above only ever constrains the CA side.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/pam.d/*
The service stanzas that load this module.
.TP
.I /lib/*/security/pam_ssoossh.so
The module, wherever
.BR libpam (8)
looks on this platform.
.SH LOGGING
Everything goes to
.BR syslog (3)
under
.BR LOG_AUTHPRIV .
Nothing is written to stdout or stderr.
.BR openlog (3)
and
.BR closelog (3)
are never called: the first mutates process\-global state and the second
closes a descriptor the host process may be holding, and inside
.BR sudo (8)
neither is this module's to touch. Every message carries its own
.B pam_ssoossh:
prefix instead.
.PP
Each attempt logs one unconditional line naming the module version and the
crypto and HTTP libraries actually linked into the process:
.PP
.RS
.nf
pam_ssoossh: 1.0.0 | crypto: OpenSSL 1.1.1k | http: libcurl/7.61.1 OpenSSL/1.1.1k
.fi
.RE
.PP
The module links crypto rather than shipping it, so which OpenSSL is
resident in
.BR sudo (8)
is a property of the host. That line is what makes the question answerable
across a fleet by grepping syslog.
.SH EXAMPLES
The documented stanza, and the only one:
.PP
.RS
.nf
auth    sufficient  pam_ssoossh.so server=https://ssoossh.example.com \\
                    trusted-ca-file=/etc/ssoossh/ca.pub \\
                    principals-map=/etc/ssoossh/principals.yaml \\
                    skew-tolerance=2s timeout=60s
auth    required    pam_unix.so
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B sufficient
means an approval ends the auth stack and anything else \(em a cancellation,
a denial, a timeout, an unreachable server \(em hands over to
.BR pam_unix (8).
A denied approval therefore reaches the password prompt. That is the
intended posture rather than an oversight: ssoossh is an additional way to
authenticate, not a gate that can lock an operator out of a host when the
browser flow is unavailable.
